00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:40.460
Yours don't.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.590
Mine doesn't either.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.820
So how do you integrate
Firebase in a seamless way

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.860
with your existing
infrastructure and back end

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.360
systems?

00:03:51.762 --> 00:03:54.220
HIRANYA JAYATHILAKA: Firebase
solves many problems for you.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.640
It's a very large and
versatile platform.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.580
But as Lauren pointed
out, Firebase, or rather

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:02.580
mobile apps, also often
have external integration

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.200
requirements.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.750
Firebase acknowledges
this fact and strives

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.620
to make it a little easier for
you to integrate your existing

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.960
systems with Firebase.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.720
To that end, we
provide a set of SDKs

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:21.000
called Admin SDKs which you can
extend your existing systems so

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.590
they [INAUDIBLE] and share data
with the Firebase platform.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.770
Alternatively, you can
use [INAUDIBLE] APIs

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.410
that we provide so your
systems can directly talk

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.140
to Firebase or [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.300
The basic idea here
is that Firebase

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.130
helps you solve many problems
related to app development.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.370
But if you ever want to
go beyond the boundaries

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:47.730
of the Firebase platform and
bring in your own systems

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:50.460
and own data and APIs
into the architecture,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.350
Firebase supports that too.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.890
So let's talk about these
different integration options

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.860
starting with the Admin SDK.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.100
Many Firebase developers,
if you've ever

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.790
written any apps with
Firebase, then you

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.860
should already be
familiar with our client

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:08.800
SDKs available for platforms
like Android, iOS, web,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:09.970
and Unity.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.760
Admin SDKs are
different from them

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.420
in the sense they are not
designed to be integrated,

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.760
or they're not designed
to be directly used,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.320
by the end users of your app.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.260
In other words, you
would never deploy

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.230
an Admin SDK on your end users
mobile device or their web

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:27.520
browser.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.050
Instead, they are meant to be
used to build [? callback ?]

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.470
[? in ?] services and
administrative tools

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.450
for your apps.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:43.540
Because of this, you will
only deploy admin SDKs

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.590
in the service that you or your
organization owns and controls.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.210
Alternatively, you
can deploy them

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.360
in managed cloud environments
like Google Cloud Functions

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.000
or App Engine, where you,
as the app developer,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.910
has control over the code.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:01.510
Admin SDKs are available in
five server side programming

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.170
languages as of today, and
we will look at some of them

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:08.060
during the upcoming demos.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:11.270
So speaking of
demos, pretty much

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.270
all the demos that we are
going to look at today

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.820
are based on a fun,
little movie rating app

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.840
that we built called FireFlicks.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.840
I think it's very fitting
since we are literally

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:22.280
sitting in a movie theater.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.090
So let's go [? to our ?]
demo machine

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:28.490
and see what this looks like.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:32.960
It's built using our JavaScript
SDK and the Vue framework.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.220
If you go to this interface,
you will see some example movies

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.470
and some rating data.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.070
All that information is stored
in Google Cloud Firestore.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:46.820
We also support several
authentication schemes

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:48.470
including Google Sign in.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:51.140
All of that is made
possible by Firebase Auth,

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.390
and this live version
of the app is coming

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.060
to you via Firebase hosting.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.330
Today you can use this interface
to add some movie reviews

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:04.220
or add them to a personal
collection for easier look up.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.560
So this looks fairly
complete at this point,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.600
but we are not quite done.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.060
In fact, throughout the rest
of the course of this talk,

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:17.270
we are going to implement
several new use cases that

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.210
will require us to
integrate what we have today

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:22.238
with a bunch of
external systems.

00:07:22.238 --> 00:07:23.530
So let's go back to the slides.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.890
Some other use cases we
are going to implement.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:36.940
First, we want to be able
to invite selected users

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.300
to join the FireFlicks
platform and become

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.030
moderators of the community.

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:46.810
We want to be able to send
out some emails containing

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.300
invitation codes to
these selected users,

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.910
and they should be able
to log in to FireFlicks

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:54.490
using those invitation codes.

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:56.290
And when they do they
should automatically

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:00.420
gain elevated
privileges in the app.

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.450
Then we would want to implement
some new backend services

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.690
to extend the
capabilities of our app,

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.010
but we would also
want to make sure

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.590
that only specific
users can actually

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.820
consume these external APIs.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.330
FireFlicks also
supports self sign up,

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.900
so you can just go to
a website and self sign

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.240
up using an email
address and a password.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.980
For such users,
we would also want

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:25.950
to run some kind of
email verification flow.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:28.117
So they should get a link
they can click and confirm

00:08:28.117 --> 00:08:30.400
their email, and we
would want to implement

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.880
that using some kind of a third
party email delivery service--

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:37.190
something like
SendGrid, for example.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.789
And finally, we want to keep
track of the movie [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:42.309
posted by the users.

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:45.370
We want to encourage users
to post, but at the same time

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:48.370
we want to prevent them
from potentially spamming

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.940
the system with too many posts.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:54.430
So let's go through these
use cases one by one

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:58.390
and see how they
are implemented.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:01.660
Let's begin with our requirement
for inviting selected users

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.150
to be moderators.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.340
We would want to
obviously pre-provision

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:08.410
these user accounts
and grant them

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.270
moderator role ahead of time.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.675
Then we would want to
be able to send them

00:09:13.675 --> 00:09:15.340
out some invitation codes.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.710
But first, let's talk
about the idea of roles

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:23.410
and how it's managed in
a platform like Firebase.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.090
In FireFlicks,
what we really want

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:28.750
is the ability to differentiate
between two classes of users--

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.940
let's say regular
users and moderators.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:32.680
Regular users can just sign up.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:35.410
They can read most of
the data on the app

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:37.696
and they can post reviews.

00:09:37.696 --> 00:09:41.710
[? Moderators, ?] on the other
hand, are by invitation only.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.390
They have read
access to everything,

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:46.570
can do pretty much everything
a regular user can,

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.600
but they also have access to
certain privileged capabilities

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:52.690
like adding new movies.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.060
In order to support
and enforce rules

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.850
correctly in an app
such as FireFlicks,

00:09:57.850 --> 00:10:00.130
we should be able to
identify the user's role

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:03.760
both in the client code and
also in the backend services

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.490
the client app depends on.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:09.680
One of the easiest ways to
do this in Firebase ecosystem

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.880
is by using a technique
called custom claims.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.340
It's built right into
the Firebase Auth flow,

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:17.320
so let's spend a
minute on figuring out

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.610
how that actually works.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:22.270
When a Firebase app kicks
off the sign-in flow,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:27.040
it sends the user's login
credentials to Firebase Auth.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.340
Firebase Auth validates them,
and if everything looks good

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:33.850
sends back an ID token
refresh token pair.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.770
At this point, the
app is signed in

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:39.430
and it can use the ID token
to call any Firebase back end

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.280
services-- services
like Firestore.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.600
The ID token is short lived.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:46.750
It expires every
hour, at which point

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:51.080
the app can use the refresh
token to gain a new ID

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.920
token for the next hour.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.470
If you build an app using
one from our client SDKs

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.553
it handles this
entire flow for you,

00:10:58.553 --> 00:11:00.220
so you don't have to
do anything really.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:03.010
It even takes care of
automatic refreshing

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:06.670
of the ID token periodically.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:12.130
The ID token itself is just
a JWT, or a JSON Web Token,

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.880
that contains some metadata
about the user encoded into it.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:18.775
There's a header, a body, and
it is signed by Firebase Auth

00:11:18.775 --> 00:11:21.550
so it cannot really be forged.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:24.790
Now what custom claims
gives us is the ability

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:28.240
to add our own custom
metadata, such as roles,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.190
into this [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:33.460
You can use the Admin SDK
to permanently associate

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:36.170
some custom claims
with a user account.

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:39.570
And then whenever that
user logs into our app,

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.520
Firebase Auth will automatically
include those custom claims

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:45.070
in the user's ID token.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:48.040
And as you can see, once
we get the roles encoded

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.200
into the user's
ID token, then we

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.980
can validate those claims in
various components of our app

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.640
to implement access control.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.270
For instance, we can
check for user's roles

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:04.780
but in our own backend
code using the Admin SDK.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:07.790
There's also a way to inspect
claims via Firebase security

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:08.290
rules.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.110
That enables you to
implement access control

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:13.120
in your Firestore,
Firebase Storage,

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.730
and Firebase Realtime
Database API calls.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:19.640
We will see examples of all
these techniques shortly.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.640
But for now, let's go
back to our original use

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.930
case of implementing a
user invitation flow.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.710
Our invitation
flow should really

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:28.000
take care of three things.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.000
It should create the
account in Firebase Auth

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.910
and grant it the
moderator role by setting

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.100
some custom [AUDIO OUT].

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:38.410
Then it should generate
an invitation code

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:41.740
for the new user and save in
some kind of a database or user

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:42.640
store.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:44.620
This way when the user
eventually logs in we

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:48.190
have a way of verifying
their invitation code.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:51.220
And finally, it should just
email out the invitation code

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:52.380
to the user.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.880
Now there are several different
ways you can implement this.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:58.150
You probably have a
user provisioning flow

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.050
in your organization.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:00.820
You can bake this
into that or you

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:05.110
can build a new administrative
tool to handle this.

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.630
What we did was to
basically build out

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:10.600
a little Python script using
the Firebase Python Admin

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:13.220
SDK to handle this entire flow.

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.760
So let's go our demo and
see how it's implemented

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:17.540
and then see how
it actually works.

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:30.310
So I have a little Python
script called create_user.py,

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.200
and one of the first things
I do in this script is just

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:37.430
importing the Admin SDK and
some of its child modules.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:38.840
And then I have
a function called

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.810
run_user_invitation_flow,
which takes the email address

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:45.780
of the target user and runs the
invitation flow for that user.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:49.190
So the first thing we do is
just initializing the Admin SDK.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:52.940
If you've written any code using
pretty much any Firebase SDK,

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:56.510
then the initialize app
API should ring some bells

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:58.520
in your head.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.050
Once we have
initialized the SDK,

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:03.440
we can use the Auth API,
specifically the create_user

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:07.330
API, to create a new user
account in Firebase Auth.

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.980
This returns an object which
contains the unique user

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:14.150
ID that Firebase
Auth has assigned.

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:17.120
And we can use that
user ID and pass it

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:20.450
to the set custom user
claims API to set some custom

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:21.950
claims on the user account.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.390
In this case, it's
just one claim

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:26.830
called moderator equals true.

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:29.950
And then we have some code here
to generate an invitation code

00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:30.640
for the user.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.220
It's just a random
string that we generate.

00:14:33.220 --> 00:14:36.430
We store all this information
in a collection called Invites

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.260
in Cloud Firestore,
so we are basically

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.480
using Cloud Firestore as
our custom user database.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.420
You can use any database
you like for this purpose.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:46.690
And finally, we have
send_invitation_email helper

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:51.610
function, which sends out this
email using a SendGrid API

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.270
call.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:58.816
So let's try this out and
see if it actually works.

00:14:58.816 --> 00:15:07.280
So I'm going to run the
script locally on my machine,

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:10.360
and I'm going to
invite Lauren to become

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:12.955
moderator of FireFlicks.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.540
So this creates the
moderator account,

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.780
grants the permissions,
and sends out the email.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:29.310
At this point, we can actually
go to Firebase Console and just

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:30.920
search for user account.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:31.980
Make sure it got created.

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.190
Yep, there's the
new user account.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:40.660
Just created today minutes ago,
and if you go to our Firestore

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.680
Console we will also
see a new invitation

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:48.307
for laurenfirebase@gmail.com.

00:15:48.307 --> 00:15:49.390
Let's go back to the demo.

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:54.700
The next step of this
use case is actually

00:15:54.700 --> 00:15:57.100
making it possible
for a user to log

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:00.897
in using one of those invitation
codes that we just emailed out.

00:16:00.897 --> 00:16:02.980
Now remember this is not
a standard authentication

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:03.480
mechanism.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:05.860
This is just something
that we are inventing.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:07.880
So since we invited
Lauren I'm going

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.760
to invite her back
here to demonstrate

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.612
how that part is implemented.

00:16:11.612 --> 00:16:12.820
LAUREN LONG: Thanks, Hiranya.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:20.080
So I've just been invited
with the Python script.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.840
I should have gotten
an email with a code--

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:24.580
an email combination.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:28.390
We will then, in our app, build
a custom authentication service

00:16:28.390 --> 00:16:32.470
to verify that this code and
email combination is valid.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.130
To do that we will be querying
our custom user store.

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:39.310
In this case, it's actually
just a Cloud Firestore database,

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:43.090
but this could be substituted
with any number of user stores

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:45.680
that would be suitable
for your application.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:49.180
After it verifies
that code is valid,

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:53.710
it then uses the Firebase Admin
SDK to generate a custom token.

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:55.780
Send that back to
the client app.

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:57.970
The client app
will use it to log

00:16:57.970 --> 00:17:03.140
in to the application with
Firebase Authentication.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:06.619
So what's underpinning
this flow is custom token,

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:10.060
which is what's generated
by the Firebase Admin SDK.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:12.250
This is what it might look like.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:16.300
You'll see that it is
signed by a service account,

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:18.609
and the most important
information it contains

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.859
is the User ID, or
the UID for short.

00:17:21.859 --> 00:17:26.170
And this UID is what will map
to the Firebase Authentication

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:30.860
user that gets created in
our project once I log in.

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.430
So to sign in with
a custom token,

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:35.830
it's done through
the client app.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:39.370
And we provide a method in
each of our client's SDK's

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.610
for them to be able to sign in
to Firebase Auth with a custom

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:43.870
token.

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.450
And once the sign
in occurs, they

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:48.850
receive an ID token
in return, and this

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:52.000
is very similar to signing
in with another provider

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:56.790
like a Facebook or a
GitHub or a Gmail sign in.

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:58.450
So let's see how
that works in action.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.990
So if we can switch
to the demo, please.

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:08.680
So here I've got my
email open, and I

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:14.210
see that I've just been
invited to join as a moderator.

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:20.950
So here I have a code which I
will copy without triggering

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:22.250
the definition.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.900
Let's now try to sign in.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:35.010
So I click on sign in with
code, with my email address,

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:37.290
and the code I just copied.

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:40.070
Click on Submit.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:43.890
It then asks me if I want to
sync it with my Google sign in.

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:46.090
I don't want to do
that at the moment.

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.570
But as you can see, I
now have a new tab called

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:51.628
Moderator that just appeared.

00:18:51.628 --> 00:18:53.420
So let's switch back
to the slides, please.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.430
So let's review
what just happened.

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:01.650
I got invited.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.720
I was emailed a code.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:08.010
I then was able to sign in
to our custom authentication

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:10.930
service, and I'll show
you that code in a moment.

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.230
And that custom
authentication service

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:17.280
then verifies that code
with a custom user store.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.300
It then generates a custom token
with the Firebase Admin SDK,

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.910
and the client app
receives that and signs

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.050
in to Firebase Authentication.

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:28.440
So if we can switch back
to the demo screen, please.

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:38.430
So our custom authentication
service is written in Go,

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:42.090
and in this case, we've
hosted on Google App Engine.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.460
But it could easily be
hosted on your own server

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:47.370
or another cloud provider.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:50.730
The method that's interesting
to us is called authenticate,

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:55.570
and it is a method that
receives a couple of parameters.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:57.510
So we have the context
of the request that

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.880
was sent, the email
of the user that's

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.400
trying to sign in as
a moderator, the code

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:06.840
that they are using, and
this method is a method off

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:08.770
of an admin client.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:12.450
So this is a client that we've
initialized with the Firebase

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.330
Admin SDK in Go.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:19.320
We first query the user
store for this email and code

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:24.580
combination, and this user store
returns that user if it exists.

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:27.330
And if it doesn't,
we get an error back

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:29.970
and we throw that error.

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:33.120
If it does exist we then
use the Firebase Amman

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.890
client that we've already
initialized previously

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:38.730
with the Auth service
of the Admin client,

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:44.220
and we generate a custom
token with the user ID.

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:47.200
So we return this custom
token back to the client,

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.980
and that's how the client was
able to sign in and verify

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:51.837
that I was a moderator.

00:20:51.837 --> 00:20:53.670
If we can switch back
to the slides, please.

00:20:56.180 --> 00:20:59.980
So this is a great way
to integrate Firebase

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:01.930
with existing user
stores that you

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:05.885
might be using like Active
Directory or LDAP or something

00:21:05.885 --> 00:21:06.385
custom.

00:21:09.602 --> 00:21:12.060
HIRANYA JAYATHILAKA: Now that
we have integrated some users

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:14.370
and rules with our
app, the next step

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.320
would be to actually
go ahead and build out

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:21.360
some new APIs and new services
to integrate our app with.

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:24.480
But also, implement some
role-based access control

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:25.950
on top of those services.

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:27.450
Specifically, we
would want to make

00:21:27.450 --> 00:21:31.350
sure that only signed in users--
that users who have signed

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:34.750
into our web app--
can access these APIs,

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:39.510
or only the signed in moderators
can access these APIs.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:42.750
Now we already know that we can
identify an authenticated user

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:43.790
by looking at the ID.

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:47.070
Token we also know that
we can inspect the custom

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.560
claims in those ID
tokens to figure out

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.840
what roles they have.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:56.220
So the easiest way to
implement access control

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.730
in your own backend code
is by just checking--

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:04.050
just decoding the ID token and
just checking what's in there.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:06.750
We get the user or the
client to send the ID token

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:09.010
with every backend request.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:10.980
We use the Admin
SDK to verify the ID

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:13.290
token or additionally
check roles,

00:22:13.290 --> 00:22:16.230
and then if the check passes,
we can go ahead and perform

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:18.960
any of the privileged
operations the user wishes

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.090
to perform on the server.

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.190
So to demonstrate
this functionality,

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.740
we are going to build
a new service that

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:28.590
enables FireFlicks
users to tweet

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:32.100
using the official
FireFlicks Twitter handle.

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:35.430
But we want to make sure that
only authenticated moderators

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:38.750
are able to access
this endpoint.

00:22:38.750 --> 00:22:41.487
We allow moderators to create
new movies in our database,

00:22:41.487 --> 00:22:43.070
so hopefully they
can use this feature

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:45.920
to tweet about new additions.

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:48.650
So the way, this is
going to be implemented

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:51.230
is we are going to put our
backend logic for actually

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:55.850
tweeting using the Twitter
API in a backend server.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.820
The user will call out to this
endpoint using the tweet that

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:00.980
needs to be published
along with the ID token,

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:02.510
and then we will verify it.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:04.070
And if everything
looks good, we'll

00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:07.990
call the Twitter API
on behalf of the user.

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:10.000
We implement this service in Go.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:14.260
In fact, it's going to be a
simple addition to the same Go

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:16.780
application that Lauren
demonstrated earlier, so it's

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:18.730
running in Google App Engine.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:20.920
A moderator can
call it from the app

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:26.200
and get the Twitter API
invoked through that.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.870
So let's go to our demo
machine again and see

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:32.020
what that code looks like.

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.210
The function that gets
triggered in this flow

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.440
is called CheckAuth.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:39.070
It receives the ID token of
the caller as an argument.

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:40.840
If you don't get
an ID tokens-- that

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.240
means if a request comes in
without a validated token--

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.940
we return an error immediately.

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:49.300
If not, we'll go ahead and
call the VerifyIDToken API

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:50.765
off the Admin SDK.

00:23:50.765 --> 00:23:52.700
This performs a
series of checks,

00:23:52.700 --> 00:23:54.640
and if everything
looks good, it actually

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:57.820
gives us a decoded
representation of the ID token.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:24:00.250
And we can query that
decoded representation

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:04.100
to check if it actually has
the moderator claim in it.

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:06.500
If not, again, we
return an error.

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:09.790
Now we wired up our web
application in a way

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:13.700
so that all incoming requests
first hit this function.

00:24:13.700 --> 00:24:16.750
And if it returns
an error [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:18.825
401 unauthorized error.

00:24:18.825 --> 00:24:20.200
Otherwise this
code will actually

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.670
go and call the Twitter API.

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:25.540
So let's see how
this code actually

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:29.630
works by first sending a
couple of fake requests.

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:31.630
So this is the
[INAUDIBLE] App Engine

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:33.000
that we are going to call.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:36.290
First, I'm going to call this
without any ID token error

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:36.790
at all.

00:24:41.150 --> 00:24:45.720
I'm basically sending this
hello would post request.

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:47.670
So as we would expect,
we get an ID token

00:24:47.670 --> 00:24:50.340
not specified error,
which is the error

00:24:50.340 --> 00:24:55.560
message that we return when
there's no ID token available.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:25:02.530
Alternatively, we can try to
send a bogus ID token header.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:06.090
Firebase ID token is the custom
header that our web [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:09.340
programmed for.

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:12.950
But this is just a fake JWT.

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:17.000
It just looks like an ID
token, but it's really not.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:21.440
So it fails to verify the token
signature, as you would expect.

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:24.430
So to get this check to pass
you have to actually call it

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:26.610
as a moderator [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.520
I'm going to use this
new tweet function.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.936
Tweet something.

00:25:34.936 --> 00:25:37.900
It says tweet posted,
so hopefully we

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:45.230
can see it on the
Twitter right now.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:45.730
There we go.

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:52.385
So let's go back to our slides.

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.020
Now we've been
telling you, you can

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:02.150
use the Admin SDK
to verify ID tokens,

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:05.150
but we haven't really told you
what happens under the hood.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:08.160
The check performed by the Admin
SDK is actually quite strong.

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:09.928
It checks for a number
of things like it

00:26:09.928 --> 00:26:12.470
has the correct signature, which
is by far the most important

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:14.510
thing, but it also
[? checks that ?] the token

00:26:14.510 --> 00:26:16.067
is not expired.

00:26:16.067 --> 00:26:21.240
[AUDIO OUT] that has to be
checked, and also things

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.660
like the token was indeed issued
against your Firebase project,

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:27.870
not coming from some random
other Firebase project.

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:29.620
And also, this is a
fairly fast operation.

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:31.680
It's not making any
RPC calls, so it

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:35.620
is OK to do this check for
every incoming request.

00:26:35.620 --> 00:26:37.410
Now one thing this
API doesn't check for

00:26:37.410 --> 00:26:40.500
is whether the ID token
is revoked or not.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:43.170
Our Admin SDKs actually
have a revoke refresh token

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:49.110
[AUDIO OUT] to users
up to that point.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:50.310
It's [AUDIO OUT].

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:56.280
Now if you want to check
if the ID token has

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.697
been revoked since
it was issued,

00:26:58.697 --> 00:27:00.030
there's a separate API for that.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:03.130
In Go it's called
VerifyIDTokenAndCheckRevoked,

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:07.530
but this actually does make
an API call or RPC call,

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:12.030
so you do have to take
that overhead into account.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:15.330
The other way you can enforce
roles and authorization

00:27:15.330 --> 00:27:18.960
in Firebase apps is by using
Firebase security rules.

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:21.510
Security rules are a
policy driven approach

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.360
to intercept API calls
made by your clients

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.360
to services like Firestore,
cloud storage, and Realtime

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:31.590
Database, and make sure that
they adhere to certain policies

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:33.720
that you've defined.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:37.340
For example, we have this
Firestore security rule

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:42.230
in place, which is what
restricts the ability

00:27:42.230 --> 00:27:45.770
to create [? new ?] [AUDIO OUT]
only the moderators.

00:27:45.770 --> 00:27:47.995
So this rule applies to
our movies collection,

00:27:47.995 --> 00:27:49.370
which is a collection
we actually

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:50.900
have in our Firestore database.

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:53.440
That's where all the movies are.

00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:56.120
As you can see, we give
the right permission

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:58.850
to only [? users ?] who
contains the moderator

00:27:58.850 --> 00:28:02.050
claim in their Auth token.

00:28:02.050 --> 00:28:05.350
This [AUDIO OUT] from
being [INAUDIBLE] create

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:06.300
or [AUDIO OUT].

00:28:11.450 --> 00:28:13.970
Moving on to our
next use case, which

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:18.210
is to be able to run an email
verification for our self

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:20.240
sign up users.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:22.850
We need to [INAUDIBLE]
to basically send a user

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:25.500
some kind of a verification
link which they can

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:27.870
confirm their email address.

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:29.930
Now interestingly,
this is actually

00:28:29.930 --> 00:28:32.810
something you can do
just in the client side

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:34.280
using our client SDKs.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:37.765
Our client SDKs provide
a sendEmailVerification

00:28:37.765 --> 00:28:39.830
[? API. ?] [? When ?]
[? you ?] call it,

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:42.900
[AUDIO OUT] email
verification flow for you.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:46.550
However, the email
sent out by this API--

00:28:46.550 --> 00:28:48.860
they use a pre-configured
set of email templates

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:53.070
and they also use a built-in
email delivery service.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:54.500
But if you're a
large enterprise,

00:28:54.500 --> 00:28:58.010
you probably would want
to customize the content

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:01.550
and layout of these emails
by including the company logo

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:04.550
and other branding information
in the emails that go out,

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:08.390
or you may have your own
email [AUDIO OUT] for things

00:29:08.390 --> 00:29:10.830
which [? you ?] want to use.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.530
So if you want that
kind of flexibility,

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:16.370
then you should consider
implementing this flow

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:19.640
as another back integration.

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:21.290
So how are we going to do it?

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:24.500
We are going to use Cloud
Functions for Firebase.

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:29.270
We are going to implement
[AUDIO OUT] every time a new

00:29:29.270 --> 00:29:34.520
user signs up to
[? FireFlicks. ?] And inside

00:29:34.520 --> 00:29:37.730
this function, we will
use [? the ?] Node.js SDK

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.370
to generate a email
[? verification ?] [AUDIO OUT],,

00:29:40.370 --> 00:29:42.680
put it into a template
of our choosing,

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:45.850
and send out using a third-party
mail delivery service--

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:48.790
SendGrid in this case.

00:29:48.790 --> 00:29:50.997
So let's go to our
demo machine and let's

00:29:50.997 --> 00:29:52.830
look at what this Cloud
Function looks like.

00:29:59.635 --> 00:30:00.135
Whoa.

00:30:09.388 --> 00:30:11.840
Thank you.

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:14.980
So we have a Cloud Function
called sendEmailVerification.

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:16.960
As you can see,
this is implemented

00:30:16.960 --> 00:30:18.820
using the Firebase
Functions SDK, which

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:22.090
is a set of extensions
that are developed on top

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:23.740
of Google Cloud Functions.

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:26.320
So we have onCreate
trigger type,

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:28.180
which gets triggered
every time a new user

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:31.090
signs up for our project.

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:32.860
This receives a
UserRecord object

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:35.560
that describes the
newly signed up user.

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:38.830
The first thing we do is
check whether this is actually

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:42.610
a password or a self sign
up user with a password.

00:30:42.610 --> 00:30:44.800
Because you see, we have
several different ways

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:47.620
users can sign up to our
app-- through Google sign in

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:49.360
or it could be a
user that we manually

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:51.340
create using our Python script.

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:52.840
So we had to first
filter those out,

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:55.630
so we have a little
check for that here.

00:30:55.630 --> 00:30:58.240
But if it is a
password user, then we

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:02.470
go ahead and call the
Admin SDK, specifically

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:04.510
the
generateEmailVerificationLink

00:31:04.510 --> 00:31:08.810
API, which gives us a URL link.

00:31:08.810 --> 00:31:10.670
Then we have the
sendEmail helper function,

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:12.310
which puts it into
our own template

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:16.300
and sends it out using SendGrid.

00:31:16.300 --> 00:31:18.340
So I'm going to go
ahead and create

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:21.590
a new account on our website.

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:25.030
I'm just going to log out
of the existing session

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:28.340
and go ahead and
click on Sign Up.

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:30.020
I'm just going to
use the same email

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:32.540
address but with a suffix.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.690
And just add a
password there, Submit,

00:31:39.690 --> 00:31:42.070
and that will actually create
the account and log me in.

00:31:42.070 --> 00:31:44.940
But as you can see, the
system recognizes that it's

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:48.540
an unverified user account.

00:31:48.540 --> 00:31:51.960
Not that event alone should have
triggered our Cloud Function,

00:31:51.960 --> 00:31:54.690
so we can go to the
Cloud Function's console

00:31:54.690 --> 00:32:00.690
or the logs in our project.

00:32:00.690 --> 00:32:05.320
Hopefully we'll see the
new event here, 1:47.

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:09.090
That's about a few seconds
ago, so the link is already

00:32:09.090 --> 00:32:12.070
in route.

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:15.800
We can probably--
oh, here we go.

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:17.950
So we now have a
verified email link

00:32:17.950 --> 00:32:19.980
sent out by our Cloud Function.

00:32:19.980 --> 00:32:24.550
We can use this link to actually
verify our user account.

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:45.090
The system recognized me
as a verified user account

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:46.683
because I clicked on the link.

00:32:46.683 --> 00:32:47.850
Let's go back to the slides.

00:32:52.010 --> 00:32:55.835
So in addition to generating
email verification links,

00:32:55.835 --> 00:32:59.170
our Admin SDKs also
have APIs for generating

00:32:59.170 --> 00:33:02.420
password reset links and
links for email sign in.

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:04.060
You can use these
APIs to further flesh

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:07.480
out your user
management landscape

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:10.450
and truly integrate your
existing systems more deeply

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:12.710
with Firebase and Firebase Auth.

00:33:12.710 --> 00:33:15.820
Now what we just saw
was just one application

00:33:15.820 --> 00:33:18.250
of Cloud Functions, and I'm
going to invite Lauren back

00:33:18.250 --> 00:33:21.515
to show other cool stuff you
can do with Cloud functions.

00:33:21.515 --> 00:33:22.390
LAUREN LONG: Awesome.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:23.860
Thanks, Hiranya.

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:26.110
So sometimes you
might want to respond

00:33:26.110 --> 00:33:29.260
to critical events happening
in our application,

00:33:29.260 --> 00:33:32.620
and Cloud Functions is a
great way of doing that.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:35.950
So the particular event
we want to respond to

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:40.120
is when a user is posting
too many reviews too quickly.

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.210
So in other words, they're
spamming the system

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:45.540
and we want a way to ban them.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:48.050
And you can do that with
the Firebase Admin SDK

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:50.550
integrated inside
of a Cloud Function.

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:54.570
You can prevent future logins
and revoke any existing refresh

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:56.020
tokens.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:57.670
Now if you've been
paying attention,

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:01.500
you might remember an ID
token lasts for an hour.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:04.980
So sometimes this could
actually take up to an hour.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:08.300
What if we want to ban
our user right away?

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.980
And that's where
security rules comes in.

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:15.540
So you might remember earlier I
talked about how Firebase uses

00:34:15.540 --> 00:34:21.270
security rules to allow granular
access control of database,

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:25.889
and we do this based on the user
ID of a user within Firebase

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:27.610
Authentication.

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:30.630
So here we've got an
example of a security rule

00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:31.830
that you can write.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:34.920
So we have a function
here called isBlackListed

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:37.409
that checks to see
if that user ID is

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:41.370
found in the blacklist
collection in Firestore.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:44.980
And we then do a match
rule for the collection

00:34:44.980 --> 00:34:48.030
where ratings are stored
to see if the user is

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:49.050
in the blacklist.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.090
If the user is in the
blacklist, then they're

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:54.300
not allowed to write a rating.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:57.570
We then also lock down
control to the blacklist

00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:00.150
to make sure nobody
can write to it.

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:03.570
Only a privileged environment
like a Cloud Function

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:06.801
can make changes to a blacklist.

00:35:06.801 --> 00:35:08.640
So let's see how all that works.

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:16.560
So here we've got our
Cloud Functions code,

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:18.710
and I'm going to
just scroll down

00:35:18.710 --> 00:35:23.030
to our function called
updateActivityIndicators, which

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:25.470
is where all of this happens.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:29.110
So this is a Firestore
triggered function.

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:32.510
It is triggered on
[? writes ?] to any ratings.

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:35.630
So in other words, when someone
has posted a new rating.

00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:38.600
We first get the
user ID, and this we

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:42.560
get from the parameter
within the database path.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:44.270
We then have a
helper function that

00:35:44.270 --> 00:35:47.570
checks this user ID to see
if they've been posting

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:50.360
a lot of reviews in
the last little bit

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:52.320
and see if we should ban them.

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:57.200
So in our demo today, we
are allowing two messages

00:35:57.200 --> 00:35:58.790
for every 2 minutes.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:00.920
And if users are
posting more than that,

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:03.530
then they will be banned.

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:06.470
If shouldBan is
true, we will then

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:11.780
update the user's user object
in Firebase Authentication

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:13.490
to be disabled.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:16.310
We will also add them to
the blacklist collection

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:17.450
in Firestore.

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:19.040
Remember, this is
the collection we're

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:21.350
checking for in
our security rules

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:25.080
to see if the user is
able to do another rating.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:27.680
And if we don't end up
needing to ban the user,

00:36:27.680 --> 00:36:29.810
we will reward them
for their review

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:33.210
by incrementing their score.

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:38.720
So let's try to see if I
can trigger the blacklist.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:40.670
So here we've got
some movies and I'm

00:36:40.670 --> 00:36:44.480
going to just start
writing some reviews.

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:49.040
So I'm going to write
a review for this one.

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:52.670
A tattooed Interpol agent
sounds like a bad movie.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:54.422
Sorry if you actually
like this movie,

00:36:54.422 --> 00:36:55.880
but I'm going to
write it one star.

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:01.710
Let's see, we've got this one.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:02.530
It's a cop movie.

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:04.360
I typically like
cop movies, so we'll

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:07.150
rate that a four
star out of five.

00:37:07.150 --> 00:37:08.740
So I've now hit my max.

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:14.370
The next one I rate should
trigger the blacklist.

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:15.972
So let's do a review.

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:23.220
So I get this review submitted.

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:24.920
Let's try to do one more.

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:33.640
So now I am banned from
submitting anymore reviews

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:37.210
as you can see from
this error message.

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:41.680
Now that was from the security
rules that we just looked at.

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:43.971
Let's see if I can log
out and log back in.

00:37:50.565 --> 00:37:52.010
So I try to log in again.

00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:56.974
I see that my account has been
disabled by the administrator.

00:37:56.974 --> 00:37:58.870
Can we switch battles
to the slides, please?

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:04.790
So one thing I do
want to note is

00:38:04.790 --> 00:38:08.250
you should not be deleting user
accounts as a way to ban them.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.310
And the reason for
that is then they

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:12.770
can just sign up again
and create a new account.

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:15.170
Whereas is if you actually
disable the account,

00:38:15.170 --> 00:38:18.437
then that prevents them
from doing further activity.

00:38:21.663 --> 00:38:23.080
HIRANYA JAYATHILAKA:
Up until now,

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:25.200
we've been using
the Admin SDKs to do

00:38:25.200 --> 00:38:27.690
all our external
integrations, but there

00:38:27.690 --> 00:38:29.340
might be times
where you would want

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:32.580
to implement an integration
without an Admin SDK.

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:34.380
Maybe your use
case is very simple

00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:36.990
and you feel like taking
a dependency on the SDK

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:38.910
is overkill, or maybe
you are programming

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:42.870
in a language for which the
Admin SDK is not available yet.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:44.760
Whatever your
reasons are, Firebase

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:48.750
strives to expose its services
via REST whenever possible so

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:51.960
you can access them
overall good old HTTPS.

00:38:51.960 --> 00:38:54.090
So as our last demo,
let's look at how

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:56.460
to implement a
simple CLI tool that

00:38:56.460 --> 00:39:00.870
interacts with the Firebase
Realtime Database over REST.

00:39:00.870 --> 00:39:03.240
Our objective is to
build a Ruby client

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:07.200
for displaying the top 10
most active FireFlicks users.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:09.450
We don't have an official
Admin SDK for Ruby yet,

00:39:09.450 --> 00:39:13.010
so this gives us an
excuse to use REST.

00:39:13.010 --> 00:39:15.960
As for tracking the
most active users,

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:18.840
remember that we already
have a workflow implemented

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:20.700
in Cloud Functions that
Lauren demonstrated

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:24.490
earlier which awards users
points for their posts.

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:26.250
So the data we are
looking for is already

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:28.780
stored in Realtime database.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:31.080
So now we are just going
to implement the lower

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:34.830
part of this diagram, where
our CLI tool securely queries

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:36.413
for the most active users.

00:39:36.413 --> 00:39:37.830
And if you're
wondering why we are

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:39.630
using Realtime Database
for this last demo,

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:41.700
it's because the REST
API for Realtime Database

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:44.010
actually supports richer
queries at the moment

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:46.800
than Firestone does.

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:51.340
So all Firebase and GCP
REST APIs support OAuth2,

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:54.040
so we need to include
an OAuth2 bearer token

00:39:54.040 --> 00:39:55.810
in all the requests we make.

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:57.550
The easiest way to
do this is by using

00:39:57.550 --> 00:39:59.590
one of the official Google
Auth libraries, which

00:39:59.590 --> 00:40:01.673
are available in about
seven different programming

00:40:01.673 --> 00:40:02.568
languages.

00:40:02.568 --> 00:40:03.860
That's what we are going to do.

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:06.075
But if you feel that's
too heavy weight,

00:40:06.075 --> 00:40:07.825
you can also code the
OAuth flow yourself.

00:40:07.825 --> 00:40:09.640
It's just a standard OAuth flow.

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:13.330
In general, if you [INAUDIBLE]
this account or a refresh token

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:17.870
you can always exchange that
for a valid OAuth2 token.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:23.780
So let's go to our demo again
and look at our Ruby code.

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:27.050
It's been a while since
I've written any Ruby code.

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:30.490
So the first thing we do is
just require the googleauth Ruby

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:34.260
gem, and then we
have some code here

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:38.170
to just initialize it out using
a Google application default

00:40:38.170 --> 00:40:39.465
credentials.

00:40:39.465 --> 00:40:42.090
And this is the URL that we are
going to make the REST call on.

00:40:42.090 --> 00:40:46.450
It's basically just to direct
URL to the Realtime Database.

00:40:46.450 --> 00:40:53.260
And we are requesting it
to order all the users

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:56.470
by their score and
give me the 10 last

00:40:56.470 --> 00:41:00.460
or the 10 highest
scores in the database.

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:02.510
And then we basically
have a big loop here

00:41:02.510 --> 00:41:05.440
which periodically makes
the same REST API call.

00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:09.910
Some code here to actually
apply the authorization header

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:12.160
to the outgoing
request and some code

00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:15.220
to parse the JSON response
coming from Realtime Database

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:19.600
and show it in a
nice little CLI view.

00:41:19.600 --> 00:41:21.440
So let's quickly try it out.

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:27.230
So here are some
fake data that's

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:29.990
coming from our database.

00:41:32.990 --> 00:41:35.510
We wanted to make sure that
your email doesn't accidentally

00:41:35.510 --> 00:41:37.410
appear here.

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:39.560
Very famous people
there and Sam Stern

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:43.520
for some reason, who's a
developer for Firebase.

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:47.260
Just to make sure that
it's working as intended,

00:41:47.260 --> 00:41:50.290
I'm going to go and just update
some data manually and see

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:52.230
if it gets picked up.

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:55.310
Realtime database.

00:41:55.310 --> 00:41:58.310
Right now Steven Spielberg
has 2,000 points.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:42:03.070
I'm going to boost his
point by a factor of 10,

00:42:03.070 --> 00:42:08.510
and you can see it jumps
up to the top right away.

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:11.422
Let's go back to
our demo slides.

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:16.440
So Realtime Database
is certainly not

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:19.630
the only Firebase product for
which there is a REST API.

00:42:19.630 --> 00:42:21.900
In fact, we are increasingly
opening up our products

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:23.460
for RESTful integrations.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:26.345
This is just a partial list
of what's available today.

00:42:26.345 --> 00:42:28.680
And the nice thing is the
way you authorize requests

00:42:28.680 --> 00:42:32.520
for all these APIs is identical
to what we demonstrated today.

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:35.010
If you know how to integrate
it with one REST API,

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:40.150
you can pretty much use any of
these other REST APIs as well.

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:43.410
Now we are approaching
the end of our talk.

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:46.680
We've been looking at a
lot of code, a lot of demos

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:49.410
in the last 15 minutes or
so, so it's a good time

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:51.240
to take a step
back and just take

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:53.310
the bird's eye view
of everything we've

00:42:53.310 --> 00:42:55.152
developed so far.

00:42:55.152 --> 00:42:57.360
We started out with a fairly
straightforward Firebase

00:42:57.360 --> 00:42:59.610
app that uses Firebase
Auth and Cloud

00:42:59.610 --> 00:43:01.983
Firestore as our database.

00:43:01.983 --> 00:43:03.900
Then we implemented a
the little Python script

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:06.420
to create new user
accounts in Firebase Auth

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:08.640
and grant them
the moderator role

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:10.770
by setting some custom claims.

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:13.710
The same script also handles
generating invitation codes

00:43:13.710 --> 00:43:17.140
and saving them into a database.

00:43:17.140 --> 00:43:21.000
Then we implemented a little
Go App Engine endpoint

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:23.610
that allows users to
log in to FireFlicks

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:27.780
using the invitation codes
they receive over email.

00:43:27.780 --> 00:43:31.290
We added a second endpoint
to that same Go application

00:43:31.290 --> 00:43:34.890
which receives the ID token
of the caller, verifies it,

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:36.690
and if it's coming
from a moderator,

00:43:36.690 --> 00:43:40.470
calls out to another
external API--

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:44.920
Twitter in this case--
to publish some tweets.

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:47.890
Then we implemented a custom
email verification flow using

00:43:47.890 --> 00:43:49.660
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:51.700
We implemented the
serverless function that gets

00:43:51.700 --> 00:43:54.310
triggered every time
a new user signs up.

00:43:54.310 --> 00:43:57.040
There we use the
Node.js Admin SDK

00:43:57.040 --> 00:44:00.640
to generate email verification
links and send them out.

00:44:00.640 --> 00:44:03.100
Then we looked at a
second Cloud Function

00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:06.370
that gets triggered for every
movie review posted by a user.

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:10.810
We use it to track
spamming behavior

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:14.860
and potentially ban such
users from the system.

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:17.410
We disable their user
accounts in Firebase Auth

00:44:17.410 --> 00:44:20.230
and use Firebase
security rules to cut off

00:44:20.230 --> 00:44:23.930
their immediate access
to our database.

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:26.950
And finally, we implemented
little Ruby CLI tool

00:44:26.950 --> 00:44:31.780
to securely query our
Realtime database over REST.

00:44:31.780 --> 00:44:34.120
Now this is certainly
a lot of moving

00:44:34.120 --> 00:44:37.360
parts implemented in several
different-- about four

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:38.440
languages.

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:41.440
Your application architecture
is probably not as complex

00:44:41.440 --> 00:44:44.690
or distributed, at
least maybe not yet.

00:44:44.690 --> 00:44:46.210
But the point we
are trying to make

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:48.040
is that if you want
to build something

00:44:48.040 --> 00:44:50.830
more advanced and
sophisticated using Firebase,

00:44:50.830 --> 00:44:51.865
you certainly can.

00:44:51.865 --> 00:44:54.760
You can start simple like
we did with just one or two

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:56.950
Firebase services and
then incrementally bring

00:44:56.950 --> 00:45:00.700
your existing systems, APIs,
and data into the architecture

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.680
and try to stay ahead of
your changing requirements.

00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:07.090
You can use things like the
Admin SDK, REST APIs, and Cloud

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:09.970
Functions to bridge
different components

00:45:09.970 --> 00:45:13.040
and make sure that you
use the same users, roles,

00:45:13.040 --> 00:45:15.710
and data both in Firebase
and everywhere else.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:16.210
Lauren.

00:45:18.980 --> 00:45:21.230
LAUREN LONG: So in
conclusion, Firebase

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:25.580
is a development platform that
allows you to build your apps,

00:45:25.580 --> 00:45:29.760
monitor their performance,
and grow your user base.

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.910
And we can integrate
with your existing

00:45:31.910 --> 00:45:36.620
systems, whether they're on-prem
or with another cloud provider.

00:45:36.620 --> 00:45:38.540
And there's two main
ways you do that.

00:45:38.540 --> 00:45:41.510
One is by using the
Firebase Admin SDK.

00:45:41.510 --> 00:45:45.600
We used that SDK in
several languages today.

00:45:45.600 --> 00:45:47.910
And you can also
use the REST APIs,

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:52.690
which you can call within
your server or any server.

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:55.090
And lastly, we also
demonstrated how

00:45:55.090 --> 00:45:58.900
you can use Cloud Functions as a
glue between different services

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:02.020
and also as a way to respond
to critical events happening

00:46:02.020 --> 00:46:03.871
in your app.

00:46:03.871 --> 00:46:07.350
All of our SDKs, including
the Firebase Admin SDKs,

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:09.240
are open source,
so we invite you

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:12.240
to be part of our community,
to file bug reports,

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:15.150
contribute, and give us
your feature requests.

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:18.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

